In article <kckdSH200WB44Ma54o@andrew.cmu.edu> ms7i+@andrew.cmu.edu (Michael Harlan Shea) writes:
>  I can't really echo too many of his ideas from personal experience
>as I've never been near BOTH an altar and any amount of monsters worth killing.
>Usually, by the time I kill them and get them over, they've spoiled.  Any
>ideas?
>
>  (-besides cursed scrolls of create monster)

Corpses will take longer to spoil if they are blessed - so if you have
a really juicy corpse (it seems to me that the Gods appear to like big
high-level corpses) that might spoil, try #dipping it in holy water.

Most monsters on the same level as the altar can be convinced to chase
me back to the altar room.  Often I can get them to stand on the altar
before I turn and fight.  Saves dragging the heavy corpse around.

If I have a pick-axe, I dig "express lanes" from the up and down staircases
to the altar so that I can get from either staircase to the altar in the
minimum number of moves.  Don't forget the advantage of a diagonal move
here...

  dig   ##          not     #######
         ##                       #
          ##                      #
           ##                     #
            ##                    #

Now I can go up or downstairs, get a monster on that level to chase me
to the stairs, kill him, and get the corpse back to the altar before
it spoils.

If there happens to be a sink on the same level as the altar, this
is a situation ripe for "abuse".   You can kick the sink and produce
black puddings to your hearts content, while enjoying sexual interludes
with the dishwasher.   Do NOT try this unless you have a rustproof
weapon (Excalibur from a fountain is rustproof) and are capable of
handling black puddings (nasty).  Black pudding corpses make good
sacrifices, and make sure to eat a few too if you are missing the
poison resistance and/or shock resistance intrinsics. 

You may have second thoughts about the dishwasher if you aren't carrying
a unicorn's horn.


